# netsurf (frontends/vita): one monotonic clock for timers, the autotest,
# the script deadline and the logs (patch 252)
# Generated 2026-10-11
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 251)
#
# The JS-fetch-stream "freeze" in the simulator (gate 250c: the http page
# dumped with every row "waiting"; 250d: 2/8): the page did not freeze.
# The frontend read the wall clock (gettimeofday) for its scheduler, the
# autotest's settle test, the JavaScript interrupt deadline and the log
# stamps, and WSL steps the wall clock forward about +1.9 s every ~32 s
# (measured: +1.871 s and +1.890 s, 32 s apart, against CLOCK_MONOTONIC).
# A step is a gap with no events in netsurf.log (198.26 -> 199.51 in
# gate 250c's log, nothing in between), and to the frontend 1.9 s of
# quiet: "settled" is 700 ms without a repaint, so a page still streaming
# (its 250 ms ticker had not yet had a chance to run) was dumped at once.
# The same step fired every timer due in the next ~2 s together (a test
# waiting 600 ms for a reconversion read the old styles) and could stop a
# script as past its deadline. performance.now() was moved to the
# monotonic clock earlier (qjs_hooks.c); the rest kept the wall clock.
#
#  - diag.c: vita_mono_us(), the frontend's clock -- the Vita's process
#    time (sceKernelGetProcessTimeWide, monotonic), CLOCK_MONOTONIC in the
#    simulator. vita_diag_ms() (settling, timeouts, log stamps) reads it.
#  - schedule.c: callbacks are timed on it.
#  - jsprobe.c: the interrupt deadline; netlog.c: its timing.
#
# On the Vita the wall clock moves when the time is set (or synced); the
# same code now ignores that too. NetSurf's own NSLOG stamps (utils/log.c)
# still read the wall clock: cosmetic.
#
# Reproduction: an LD_PRELOAD shim steps gettimeofday / CLOCK_REALTIME
# +1.9 s every 2.5 s; autotest pass N with JS-fetch-stream six times --
# see docs/phase-0/10-hardware-test-plan.md, "Since run 30".
